title: Your own workspace
intro: Put the editor's tabs in the order you want, close the panels you don't need, and keep what you're working on in the middle.
project: Platformer | Workspace

chapter: Order and close tabs | Drag a tab along its group, or close it
explain: panel:Assets | Tabs in any order | The panels under the scene are tabs: Assets, Output, Profiler, Tables, Simulations and Chart.
movetab: Profiler > 0
expectlayout: Hierarchy | (Documents / Profiler, Assets, Output, Tables, Simulations, Chart) | Inspector
explain: panel:Profiler | Moved first | Drag a tab along its group's tabs, and a box shows the gap it will go in.
closepanel: Simulations
closepanel: Chart
expectlayout: Hierarchy | (Documents / Profiler, Assets, Output, Tables) | Inspector; closed: Simulations, Chart
say: Not making a rhythm game, or balancing with simulations? Close those panels with the cross on their tabs, or a middle-click.
explain: menu:View | View, Panels | Every panel is listed under View, Panels. Pick a closed one and it opens again where it started.
menu: View > Panels > Chart
expectlayout: Hierarchy | (Documents / Profiler, Assets, Output, Tables, Chart) | Inspector; closed: Simulations
explain: panel:Chart | Back again | Saved layouts remember the order of the tabs and which panels are closed, and Reset Layout opens them all again.

chapter: Panels in the middle | Drop a panel among the documents
say: The Tables panel is a spreadsheet, and it wants room.
dock: Tables > Documents
expectlayout: Hierarchy | (Documents: Tables / Profiler, Assets, Output, Chart) | Inspector; closed: Simulations
explain: panel:Tables | Among the documents | Dropped on the Scene and Game tabs, or in the middle of the scene, a panel becomes a tab beside them, filling the whole area.
dock: Tables > Profiler
expectlayout: Hierarchy | (Documents / Profiler, Assets, Output, Chart, Tables) | Inspector; closed: Simulations
explain: panel:Tables | And back | Drag its tab out again to put it back among the panels. Layouts remember where it was.

chapter: A table in a tab | Data > Open Table in Tab
say: Or open just one table in a tab of its own.
menu: Data > New Table...
prompt: Loot
menu: Data > Open Table in Tab > Loot
explain: doctab:Loot | Its own tab | Data, Open Table in Tab puts a table beside the scene, the same spreadsheet as the Tables panel's, with all the room of the middle.
click: tables:addrow
explain: tables:grid | Edited the same way | Edits are saved as they're made, and undo takes them back. The Data menu works on the table you used last, here or in the panel.
explain: menu:Data | Open Tables in Tabs | Turn on Open Tables in Tabs, and opening or double-clicking a table in Assets opens it in a tab like this.
closedocument: Loot

chapter: Editors in tabs | The Sprite Painter and Sound Maker beside the scene
say: The asset editors open in the middle too, as tabs beside the scene, not in windows over it.
menu: Art > New Sprite...
painter
click: painter:idea:Creature
explain: doctab:Sprite Painter | A tab of its own | The Hierarchy and Inspector stay beside it. A dot on its tab says it has changes that aren't saved, and closing it asks, as a scene does.
artname: critter
click: painter:save
expectart: Sprites/critter.png
menu: Audio > New Sound...
maker
click: maker:preset:Coin
explain: doctab:Sound Maker | One tab each | Every picture and sound you open gets a tab of its own. Open one that's already open, and its tab comes forward.
click: maker:save
closedocument: Sound Maker
closedocument: critter.png
explain: scene | Back to the scene | Close a tab with its cross, or a middle-click. The Clip Editor, the Move List and the Dialogue Editor open as tabs too.
